steps.body:
Most input problems start the same way: read the text, split it into words, then use the array methods you already know. Split on `/\s+/` after `trim()`, so doubled spaces and the trailing newline never turn into empty words:

```javascript
const words = input.trim().split(/\s+/);
const caps = words.map((w) => `${w[0].toUpperCase()}${w.slice(1)}`);
console.log(caps.join(" "));
console.log(words.toSorted((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b)).join(", "));
```

`map` transforms each word and `join` glues the results back into one line. `toSorted` returns a sorted copy and leaves `words` in input order, which matters when a later line still needs the original order. To pick one word, `reduce` with the first word as the starting value and a strict `>` or `<`, so the earliest word wins ties.

**The gotchas:** `split(" ")` turns `"a  b"` into `["a", "", "b"]`. And empty input still gives one word, because `"".split(/\s+/)` is `[""]`.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const words = input.trim().split(/\s+/);

const capitalized = words.map((w) => `${w[0].toUpperCase()}${w.slice(1)}`);
const reversed = words.toReversed();
const sorted = words.toSorted((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));
const longest = words.reduce((best, w) => (w.length > best.length ? w : best), words[0]);

console.log(`Capitalized: ${capitalized.join(" ")}`);
console.log(`Reversed: ${reversed.join(" ")}`);
console.log(`Sorted: ${sorted.join(", ")}`);
console.log(`Longest: ${longest}`);

