steps.body:
Input keys belong in a Map, not a plain object, because a word like `constructor` would collide with inherited object keys. With the words read by `input.trim().split(/\s+/)`, count them with `get` and `??`, and use a Set when you only care whether a value appeared:

```javascript
const counts = new Map();
for (const w of words) {
  counts.set(w, (counts.get(w) ?? 0) + 1);
}
const unique = new Set(words); // unique.size is the distinct count
```

A Map iterates in insertion order, which follows the input, not the order your output needs. To print by a rule such as highest count first, spread it into `[key, value]` pairs and sort them. Destructure the pairs in the comparator and add a tie-breaker with `||`:

```javascript
const sorted = [...counts].sort(
  ([wordA, countA], [wordB, countB]) => countB - countA || wordA.localeCompare(wordB)
);
```

`countB - countA` puts bigger counts first. When the counts are equal it is 0, which is falsy, so `||` falls through to the alphabetical comparison. Spread a Set the same way, `[...set]`, before you sort or join it.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const words = input.trim().split(/\s+/);

const unique = new Set(words);
const counts = new Map();
for (const w of words) {
  counts.set(w, (counts.get(w) ?? 0) + 1);
}

const sorted = [...counts].sort(
  ([wordA, countA], [wordB, countB]) => countB - countA || wordA.localeCompare(wordB)
);

console.log(`Unique words: ${unique.size}`);
for (const [word, count] of sorted) {
  console.log(`${word}: ${count}`);
}

