practice.solution:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const names = input.trim().split(/\s+/);

const unique = new Set(names);
const counts = new Map();
for (const name of names) {
  counts.set(name, (counts.get(name) ?? 0) + 1);
}

const repeats = [...counts]
  .filter(([, count]) => count > 1)
  .map(([name]) => name)
  .sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));

console.log(`Unique visitors: ${unique.size}`);
console.log(`Repeat visitors: ${repeats.length > 0 ? repeats.join(", ") : "none"}`);

steps.body:
When each line is a record, split it and destructure the fields straight into names. Everything arrives as text, so convert the numeric fields with `Number`. Then hand them to a class, so the rest of the program works with real objects and methods. Here `lines` is `input.trim().split("\n")`, and `Item` is a class with `name`, `price`, and `qty` fields and a `total()` method:

```javascript
const n = Number(lines[0]);
const items = lines.slice(1, n + 1).map((line) => {
  const [name, price, qty] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  return new Item(name, Number(price), Number(qty));
});
```

Declare the class above this code, because classes are not hoisted. A rest pattern such as `const [name, ...scores] = parts` collects a variable number of trailing fields.

**The gotcha:** never sort on raw float results. `1.1 * 3` is `3.3000000000000003`, so it sorts above an exact `3.3` even though both print as `3.30`. Sort on the value you display instead:

```javascript
items.sort((a, b) =>
  Math.round(b.total() * 100) - Math.round(a.total() * 100) || a.name.localeCompare(b.name)
);
```

Rounding to whole cents makes the two totals equal, so the name tie-breaker decides, just as the reader expects. For values shown with 1 decimal, compare `Math.round(value * 10)` the same way.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");
const n = Number(lines[0]);

class Item {
  constructor(name, price, qty) {
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
    this.qty = qty;
  }

  total() {
    return this.price * this.qty;
  }
}

const items = lines.slice(1, n + 1).map((line) => {
  const [name, price, qty] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  return new Item(name, Number(price), Number(qty));
});

items.sort((a, b) =>
  Math.round(b.total() * 100) - Math.round(a.total() * 100) || a.name.localeCompare(b.name)
);

for (const item of items) {
  console.log(`${item.name} x${item.qty} = ${item.total().toFixed(2)}`);
}
const grand = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.total(), 0);
console.log(`Grand total: ${grand.toFixed(2)}`);

